# kernel-patches-k1-k4.upstream.patch

Sprint 029 ticket 001 (K1-K4: post-floor twist-hold reference, stale-tick
freeze, anti-windup, rearmReferences()), with K1 CORRECTED by sprint 029
ticket 010 (the twist-hold reference now integrates the floored
COMMANDED twist, never the post-floor TRIMMED targets -- see the K1
bullet in step 3 below). Design: docs/design/motion-profile-unification.md
§4.5. Issue: clasi/sprints/029-motion-profile-unification-one-shaper-one-floor-predictive-arrival/issues/kernel-reference-handling-twist-floor-stale-tick-antiwindup.md.

## What this is

A `git diff` of THIS repo's own vendored copy
(`src/core/diffdrive.{h,cpp}`) between the pre-sprint-029 baseline
(commit 13450ef, the parent of ticket 001's own e207cc0) and the CURRENT
state -- produced by `git diff 13450ef -- src/core/diffdrive.h
src/core/diffdrive.cpp` on the sprint branch, after ticket 010 landed
the K1 correction. It is the exact, tested change (K1-K4 as they stand
today, not ticket 001's own first landing); nothing here is hand-written
or re-derived. Regenerated from the same baseline commit ticket 001 used,
per that ticket's own convention -- see this file's own git history for
the version staged after ticket 001 alone, before the K1 correction.

## How to apply it upstream

Upstream is `radio-robot-elite/src/firm/diffdrive/differential_drive.{h,cpp}`
(read-only from this worktree -- this ticket does not, and must not, edit
that repository directly). Per the paired-PR default (design §12, issue
`decide-the-kernel-fork.md`; the stakeholder had not ruled on the fork
question as of ticket 001's start):

1. In a `radio-robot-elite` checkout, open
   `src/firm/diffdrive/differential_drive.h` and
   `src/firm/diffdrive/differential_drive.cpp` side by side with this
   patch.
2. The hunks below will very likely NOT apply cleanly with `patch -p1` or
   `git apply`: upstream's own header/setter comments already diverge
   from this repo's vendored copy (confirmed 2026-09-03 by diffing the
   two trees directly -- upstream carries a fuller "Non-finite in =
   refused, and RECORDED..." comment on every setter that this repo's
   copy dropped when it was vendored), so the CONTEXT lines around each
   hunk will not match even though the CODE they touch is
   byte-identical. Apply each hunk BY HAND: locate the same function
   (`rebasePosition()`, `step()`, `controlStep()`, `positionError()`,
   `applySpeedFloor()`, the `DifferentialDrive` class body for the
   header) and make the same edit, adapting only comment wording that
   upstream already phrases differently.
3. The four patches, in the order they appear below:
   - K1 (`controlStep()`), CORRECTED 2026-09-04 (sprint 029 ticket 010):
     decide `twistHoldActive` up front so the same condition governs
     both `trim` and whether the reference integrates; have
     `applySpeedFloor()` report the scale it applied as a new
     `float& floorScale` out-parameter (1.0 when the floor does not
     bind) and store it (`lastFloorScale_`, plus a `lastFloorScale()`
     diagnostic accessor); source the twist-hold `headroom` clamp from
     `lastSpeedLeft_`/`lastSpeedRight_` (the PREVIOUS cycle's floored
     speeds); AFTER `applySpeedFloor()` runs, integrate
     `twistRef_.reference += (0.5f * (scaledRight - scaledLeft)) *
     floorScale * dt` -- `scaledLeft`/`scaledRight` computed BEFORE
     `trim` is folded in, never the post-floor `speedLeft`/`speedRight`
     (which already have `trim` folded in). Ticket 001's own first
     landing integrated `0.5f * (speedRight - speedLeft) * dt` of those
     TRIMMED targets instead: the twist-hold servo's own `trim` output
     fed back into the reference it is judged against next tick, a
     positive-feedback loop ideal (matched) wheels never excite but a
     real per-wheel mismatch does -- MEASURED on tovez
     (`captures/bench-acceptance-029-20260904c/`), `WHEELS_V 200 200`
     drove one wheel negative and the other to 492 mm/s under that
     landing (reports/bench-acceptance-029-20260904c.md §6). With
     `vMin = 0` (this design's own fleet bake, K5) `floorScale` is
     always 1.0 and the corrected line reduces to the ORIGINAL
     pre-K1-patch line, `scaledTwist * dt` -- which was right; only the
     floor-bound case (ticket 001's own MEASURED -11% reverse duty on a
     cruise-100 pivot) needed a fix, and the corrected form still fixes
     it via `floorScale`.
   - K2/K3 (`positionError()`): add the `bool advanced` parameter (and
     update its declaration in the header and both call sites in
     `controlStep()` to pass `sampleAdvancedLeft_`/`sampleAdvancedRight_`);
     gate the `ref.reference += speed * dt` line on `advanced`; add the
     post-update anti-windup clamp on `ref.reference` itself when
     `posErrMax > 0`.
   - K2's own bookkeeping (`step()`): add the `sampleAdvancedLeft_`/
     `sampleAdvancedRight_` member fields (header) and set them from the
     existing `stampLeftBefore`/`stampRightBefore` vs
     `sampleLeft_.sampleTime`/`sampleRight_.sampleTime` comparison
     `step()` already computes for `i2cFaultCount_`.
   - K4 (`rearmReferences()`): new public method + `rearmReq_`/
     `seenRearmReq_` counter pair (header, same shape as
     `rebasePosition()`/`rebaseReq_`/`seenRebaseReq_`); consume it at the
     top of `step()`, before `controlStep()`, disarming
     `posRefLeft_.armed`/`posRefRight_.armed`/`twistRef_.armed`.
   - The four diagnostic accessors on `DifferentialDrive`
     (`twistReferenceCounts()`, `twistReferenceArmed()`,
     `positionReferenceCounts()`, and ticket 010's own
     `lastFloorScale()`) are host-test support only (this repo's
     `tests/host/test_kernel_reference_handling.py`), not part of the
     control law. Upstream may adopt them for its own test coverage of
     the same patches, or omit them -- they have no effect on behavior
     either way.
4. After applying, run upstream's own fidelity/host-test suite for
   `differential_drive.{h,cpp}` (per this repo's own
   `tests/host/test_kernel_harness.py` pattern -- confirm the equivalent
   exists there) and confirm it stays green, the same bar these tickets
   held themselves to in this repo (`tests/host/test_kernel_harness.py`,
   `test_motion_engine_primitives.py`, `test_motion_engine_reductions.py`,
   `test_motion_engine_estop_and_refusal.py`,
   `test_regression_yaw_taper_pure_turn.py`,
   `test_motion_engine_acceleration_profile.py`,
   `test_frozen_encoder_hold.py`, `test_kernel_reference_handling.py`
   and `test_profile_probe_kernel.py`/`test_profile_probe.py` -- the
   last two now also cover the asymmetric-wheel/lag regression ticket
   010 added).
5. Record the upstream PR/commit reference back on ticket 001
   (`clasi/sprints/029-.../tickets/001-...md`) once it lands. As of
   ticket 010's own close, the upstream PR is still NOT opened -- this
   file is the artifact that unblocks opening it, not evidence it has
   landed.

